WEBVTT
TITLE Monitors and Condition Variables: the While Rule, Signal against Broadcast, and the Semaphore Construction — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.000
A monitor lets one thread inside at a time. That is mutual exclusion, and it comes free.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:12.000
T1 is inside and needs the buffer to be non-empty, but only T2 can put an item in it.

3
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:18.000
Busy-waiting inside the monitor spins forever, because the spinner is holding the door shut.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:24.000
So the thread steps out: release the lock, join a named queue, and sleep.

5
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:30.000
That queue is a condition variable. The lock solves exclusion; the queue solves waiting.

6
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
Part one is private data. It is reachable only through the monitor’s own procedures.

7
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Part two is the entry procedures. Only one thread may be inside any of them at once.

8
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:51.000
Part three is the implicit lock: taken on entry, released on exit, and released again around every wait.

9
00:00:51.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Part four is the condition variables: one queue each, no value, and three operations.

10
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:07.000
wait does three things as one indivisible action: release the lock, join the queue, sleep.

11
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:14.000
On waking it re-acquires the lock before returning, so the code after the wait is protected.

12
00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:21.000
If the release and the sleep could be separated, a signal arriving in the gap would be lost.

13
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:30.000
One signal moves exactly one thread from the queue to runnable. It does not hand it the lock.

14
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:37.000
A semaphore has a value. A condition variable has only a queue.

15
00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:44.000
P blocks only when the value is zero. wait always blocks, whatever the state.

16
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:51.000
V adds one to the value when nobody waits. signal with nobody waiting does nothing at all.

17
00:01:51.000 --> 00:02:00.000
And a condition variable may only be used while holding the lock. A semaphore has no such rule.

18
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:07.000
Hoare semantics: the signaller hands over the lock and the processor to the waiter.

19
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:14.000
Under Hoare the condition is still true when the waiter returns, so an if is enough.

20
00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:21.000
Mesa semantics: the signaller keeps the lock, and the waiter is only made runnable.

21
00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:30.000
So under Mesa the predicate may be false on return, and the loop is required. The cost is two switches against none.

22
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:37.000
One producer, two consumers, one slot. remove is written twice, with while and with if.

23
00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:44.000
Every interleaving of every statement is enumerated, so the answer is a count and not an opinion.

24
00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:51.000
The if version reaches ninety-two states, and six of them take an item that is not there.

25
00:02:51.000 --> 00:03:00.000
C1 sleeps, the producer wakes it, C2 enters first and takes the item, and C1 resumes inside its if.

26
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:07.000
A pool of ten free units, two waiters needing sixteen and six, and one signal.

27
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:14.000
The signal wakes the thread needing sixteen, which re-waits, and the wake-up is spent.

28
00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:21.000
The thread needing six is asleep although ten units are free. Nothing can ever wake it.

29
00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:28.000
Broadcast wakes both, the thread needing six takes six, and the run finishes.

30
00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Signal is right when every waiter on that variable can be satisfied by the same change.

31
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:37.000
A Hoare monitor can be built from three semaphores and two counters.

32
00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:44.000
wait parks on x_sem after releasing the lock. signal must release that same semaphore.

33
00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:51.000
All four candidate answers are executed. Three stall and one finishes.

34
00:03:51.000 --> 00:04:00.000
So the answer is the pair that names x_sem in both blanks: P on the waiter side, V on the signaller side.

35
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:08.000
The monitor buffer needs two condition variables, one for each condition.

36
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:15.000
Both waits sit in while loops, and the signal comes after the change, inside the monitor.

37
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:23.000
The four counts: at most N items, at most P producers asleep, at most C consumers asleep, and P plus C in total.

38
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:30.000
The same three names in Java, POSIX threads, C# and Go: the lock, and the condition.

39
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:38.000
Four steps: name the lock and the queues, then read who runs after the signal.

40
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:45.000
Check the predicate, not the name. A condition variable has no value to test.

41
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:53.000
Four traps: signal is not V, wait is not P, a wake-up is not a promise, broadcast is not waste.

42
00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:00.000
A monitor gives exclusion for free; a condition variable gives waiting; every wait is re-tested in a loop.

